Modern web applications increasingly rely on client-side storage, yet existing browser taint-tracking systems lose security context once data is serialized and stored persistently. This limitation weakens the detection of long-lived information flows and storage-based attacks. We extend the Foxhound research browser with persistent taint-tracking that preserves taint metadata across client-side storage, enabling continuous monitoring of data flows across both runtime execution and persistent storage. We evaluate our system using the MotionMark 1.3.1 and JetStream 2.2 benchmark suites. The results show that although dynamic taint-tracking introduces a 28-40% slowdown compared to a standard browser, the additional overhead of persistence is small (approximately 1-5% over the original Foxhound system). Object and string-intensive workloads experience moderate slowdowns, while numeric and WebAssembly workloads remain largely unaffected. Our findings demonstrate that persistent taint-tracking is practical for real-world deployment, strengthening browser defenses against storage-based attacks while maintaining acceptable performance.